{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfd6oH3GtUM1i2PpWfVv6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the repair candidate with a genuinely new mechanism, and it passes the self-check. The check doesn't test novelty against the live set, so whether it clears that gate is only known at admission.\n\n**Why the first attempt was rejected:** its code was line for line the same as the live model `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`. Only the comments differed, so its predictions matched exactly (RMSE 0.00000 on the novelty pool).\n\n**The new candidate, `longest_alternation_asymmetric_ideal`:**\n- **Base model:** `run_variety_asymmetric_ideal_rate`, rank 1 and statistically tied with the best. Everything it already had stays: each person's own ideal switching rate and sensitivity to it, with switching too often judged more leniently than switching too rarely; preference for varied run lengths; personal weights on heads/tails imbalance and the longest streak; shared weights on the final streak and mirror symmetry; and a personal guessing rate.\n- **The one change:** people also notice the longest unbroken stretch of strict alternation (HTHTH…), as the alternation counterpart of the longest streak. It shifts how random a sequence looks by one shared weight.\n- **What it predicts:** two sequences with the same number of switches are judged differently when one bunches its switches into a long rigid alternating stretch (HHTHTHTT) and the other spreads them out (HTHHTTHT).\n\n**Check result:** it loaded, had a finite log-probability, met the data contract and completed the smoke fit, with ELPD-LOO −3567.8. The check warned that PSIS-LOO was unreliable: 8.2% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. It also couldn't report R-hat from a single chain. Both warnings come from the 100-draw, one-chain smoke fit. Admission's full fit will decide whether the estimate is reliable and whether the mode